Cap oracle: dummy-bootchain workaround removed; limine.exe block root-caused
============================================================================

Date: 2026-10-02
Base commit: 711ea46 "C2: check the manifest schema against the spec, and fix
              what it found"
Files changed: tools/gen-cap-manifest.py, kernel/scripts/test-cap.sh,
               docs/sac-build-blocks.md, progress.md

1. The workaround, and why it is gone
-------------------------------------
df69025 made `bootchain` mandatory at the top level of a manifest, contradicting
docs/manifest-format.md:116 ("An optional `bootchain` object"). Two callers were
written to live with that bug by fabricating a bootchain:

  tools/gen-cap-manifest.py   built a dummy limine.bin + kernel.bin and passed
                              bootchain= to build_manifest()
  kernel/scripts/test-cap.sh  built the same dict a second time for its
                              A-negative section

Its own comment said so, and named the constraint explicitly:

    # The C2 verifier in this tree requires the bootchain section in every
    # manifest (_TOP in nova_trust.py), although docs/manifest-format.md
    # calls it optional. Not this work order's bug to fix (AGENTS.md rule
    # 11); the generator complies with the code by carrying a deterministic
    # dummy bootchain whose files live beside the package.

344344a removed the requirement, so both the workaround and its justification
became false. Both are deleted here; the comment now explains why the section is
absent, citing the doc line, instead of describing a constraint that no longer
exists.

The section is genuinely optional, and omitting it is not a shortcut: the doc
says the section is present when the manifest boots a system, "not for
daily-driver software updates", and this oracle's package is a daily-driver
package. So this also moves the oracle onto the bootchain-absent path, which
nothing exercised before.

2. The oracle still passes, through the real pipeline
-----------------------------------------------------
$ py -3 tools/gen-cap-manifest.py --out build/cap-oracle
gen-cap-manifest: vocabulary from kernel\src\cap.rs (7 grantable):
  0: device:camera
  1: device:microphone
  2: device:audio
  3: fs:read <- subject grant
  4: fs:write
  5: compute:expression
  6: compute:verify
gen-cap-manifest: signed manifest for subject caps ['fs:read'] -> build\cap-oracle\manifest.json

$ py -3 tools/verify-manifest.py build/cap-oracle/manifest.json \
    --public-key build/cap-oracle/root.pub --files build/cap-oracle \
    --state build/cap-oracle/state.json
verify-manifest: build\cap-oracle\manifest.json against key sha256:cf9d7c988c8e5d4e6f68a24a7a1cdfbaa192e77ad079e00f96d0a9228be521ff
  ok   signature valid; release 0.1.0, release_sequence 1
  ok   structure valid; newer than last accepted 0
  ok   1 package file(s) match size and sha256
verify-manifest: PASS

and the manifest really has no bootchain key:

$ py -3 -c "import json;m=json.load(open('build/cap-oracle/manifest.json',encoding='utf-8'));print('top-level keys:',sorted(m));print('has bootchain:','bootchain' in m)"
top-level keys: ['manifest_version', 'packages', 'release', 'signature', 'trust']
has bootchain: False

This is the first bootchain-less manifest to verify end to end since df69025,
and it is the case the doc describes as the common one.

3. Full oracle run: layers A and A-negative pass, B+C blocked
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ bash kernel/scripts/test-cap.sh > build/cap-after.log 2>&1; rc=$?; echo "TEST_CAP_RC=$rc"
TEST_CAP_RC=1

Layer A and A-negative, from build/cap-after.log:

--- cap oracle A: the vocabulary is declarable in a signed C2 manifest
verify-manifest: PASS
--- cap oracle A-negative: wildcards and out-of-grammar rows are refused
  ok    refused unsigned: 'device:camera:*'
  ok    refused unsigned: 'device:*'
  ok    refused unsigned: '*:*'
  ok    refused unsigned: '*'
  ok    refused unsigned: 'fs:read:extra'
  ok    refused unsigned: 'Camera'
  ok    refused unsigned: 'device:Camera'
  ok    refused unsigned: 'not-a-capability'
  ok    refused unsigned: ''
  ok    grammar-valid unknown row verifies end-to-end (vocabulary enforcement lives in the kernel)
  ok    tampered signature rejected end-to-end (rc=1)

Layer B+C, the guest half:

--- cap oracle B+C: boot the selftest image and recompute every probe
  FAIL  selftest boot did not exit cleanly (rc=124; 35 = a gate failed)

rc=1 comes only from that guest half. It is the limine.exe block in section 4,
not this change: layers A and A-negative never touch QEMU, and both are green
with the workaround gone.

A note on the numbers: the pipeline in an earlier attempt reported rc=0 for this
script because the exit status captured was `head`'s, not the script's. The
transcript above redirects to a file and captures rc on its own line, so
TEST_CAP_RC=1 is the script's real verdict.

4. The limine.exe block: root cause, and why it is not fixable here
-------------------------------------------------------------------
4a. It is not transient, so a retry wrapper would not help.

scripts/cargo-retry.sh exists because the rust-lld block clears on retry. That
property does NOT hold for limine.exe:

$ for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do "$T" bios-install build/retry.hdd >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?; echo "attempt $i: rc=$rc"; done
attempt 1: rc=126
attempt 2: rc=126
attempt 3: rc=126
attempt 4: rc=126
attempt 5: rc=126

Five consecutive attempts, all rc=126. No retry budget would ever get through.

4b. The Windows-level verdict names the cause.

$ cmd //c "...\limine-tool-windows-x86\limine.exe"
'...\limine.exe' was blocked by your organization's Device Guard policy.
Contact your support person for more info.

and the CodeIntegrity event that corresponds to it, read with
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Microsoft-Windows-CodeIntegrity/Operational'; Id=3033}:

02 October 2026 19:13:15
Code Integrity determined that a process (\Device\HarddiskVolume3\Windows\System32\cmd.exe)
attempted to load \Device\HarddiskVolume3\Users\surya\Documents\dont-share-personal-info\.freebuff\ref\limine\limine-binary\limine-tool-windows-x86\limine.exe
that did not meet the Enterprise signing level requirements.

The companion event 3118 at the same timestamp is titled "Smart App Control Block
Deteails" [sic]. So this is the Smart App Control / CodeIntegrity mechanism that
docs/sac-build-blocks.md already documents, reached through a different symptom.

Correction to what I wrote earlier: the previous log
(docs/logs/manifest-schema-drift.log, section 9) said this was "NOT the failure
documented in docs/sac-build-blocks.md". That was wrong, and it was wrong for
the reason AGENTS.md warns about - I compared error signatures (rc=126 exec
denial versus rust-lld's os error 4551) and inferred different causes, when the
event log shows the same enforcing mechanism. The signatures differ; the cause
does not. docs/sac-build-blocks.md now carries both cases.

4c. The documented workaround does not apply either.

- The C-compiler fallback in build-disk.sh cannot help: there is no compiler on
  this host ($ command -v cc gcc clang -> no output), and a freshly compiled
  binary is unsigned too, so it would fail the same "Enterprise signing level"
  requirement.
- Turning SAC off is a machine-owner decision; scripts/sac-build-blocks.md
  already states the repo treats a persistent block as a hard build failure and
  never turns SAC off itself. Nothing here changes that.

So the block is not fixable inside this repository. It needs either a signed
limine.exe or a machine-owner policy change.

4d. build-disk.sh is honest about it (checked because I expected otherwise).

I expected the script to print "BIOS stages installed" and carry on, since the
call site is `"$TOOL" bios-install "$OUT"` with the success echo on the next
line. It does not: `set -euo pipefail` aborts the script at that line.

$ grep -c 'BIOS stages installed' build/check-full.log
0
$ bash scripts/build-disk.sh release >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?; echo "BUILD_DISK_RC=$rc"
BUILD_DISK_RC=126

No false success is claimed anywhere, and the script's exit code is 126, which
check.sh stage 3 turns into FAILED=1. My hypothesis was wrong; nothing needs
fixing there, and no change was made. A friendlier message naming SAC would be
nicer than a bare "Permission denied", but that is polish, not a correctness
defect, so it is not in this commit.

5. What was NOT verified
------------------------
Layer B+C of the cap oracle (the guest recomputation of every CAP_KERNEL_PROBE
line, and the done-when denial evidence) did not run and is not claimed. It is
gated on an image with BIOS boot stages, which cannot be built on this host
while limine.exe is blocked. That layer was green at commit 65e0270, before the
block appeared; it has not been green since, and this change did not touch it.
CI on Linux is where it can run.
